    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8829a7c3_9798585c",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 107,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T22:04:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, the faults would not clear unless the condition is gone. For instance, if you reboot the EC while charging at 20V; the initialization of this will reconfigure the OVP limit to 5V and you\u0027ll get about 1000 messages saying OVP until it discharges below 5V.\n\nThe real problematic one is OCP, since we count the overcurrent events and will permanently disable the port if there are too many. It takes a few ms after the interrupt for the OCP to go away I\u0027ve found (not obvious from the datasheet), so if there is ever an overcurrent we will permanently disable the port because we will get like 100 OCP alerts since it never clears. One other solution I thought of is delaying a bit between each time we re-check the interrupts, the issue is that we could miss an FRS event and violate timing if we do that. This may be an unrealistic corner case though.",
      "parentUuid": "d264ea91_c2389212",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 107,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 107,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
